Levels of Write Concern :
------------------------------ >

MongoDB offers several levels of write concern:

w: 0 (Unacknowledged): The database doesn't send any acknowledgment for the write operation. This is faster but risky because you won't know if the write succeeded.

w: 1 (Acknowledged): The database acknowledges that the write operation has been applied to the primary replica. This is the most commonly used level.

w: majority (Majority Acknowledgment): The database ensures the write has been applied to the majority of nodes in a replica set. This offers higher reliability.

Custom Levels (w: n): You can specify the number of replica set members that must acknowledge the write. For example, w: 2 requires acknowledgment from the primary and one other replica.

Durability with Journaling
Write concern also interacts with journaling, which ensures that write operations are saved to disk (even if the system crashes). Setting j: true enables journaling, providing an extra layer of durability for your data.

Write Concern in Transactions
When using transactions, you need to set an appropriate write concern to ensure the reliability of all write operations within the transaction. By default, MongoDB uses w: majority for transactions, ensuring that changes are acknowledged by the majority of replica set members. You can customize the write concern if needed.

Example in Transactions
Imagine you have a transaction that:

Adds a new order to the orders collection.

Updates inventory in the inventory collection.

Records payment details in the payments collection.

If the write concern is set to w: majority, MongoDB will ensure that all these operations are successfully applied and acknowledged by the majority of nodes. If any of the write operations fail to meet this level of acknowledgment, the entire transaction is aborted to maintain data consistency.

Why is Write Concern Important?
Data Integrity: Ensures your writes are properly saved and acknowledged.

Fault Tolerance: Protects against system failures or crashes during a write operation.

Performance Control: Allows you to balance reliability and speed based on your application's needs.


