@@## Acquiring the Looks in mongo db :
=========================================================== > 


.> Acquiring Locks
By default, transactions wait up to 5 milliseconds to acquire locks required by the operations in the transaction. If the transaction cannot acquire its required locks within the 5 milliseconds, the transaction aborts.

 Locking in MongoDB :
------------------------------- >

What’s Happening? :
-------------------------------> 

..> Imagine a transaction is like a person trying to enter a room (the “lock”) to perform an important task. The room can only be accessed by one person at a time. The transaction waits for a few moments (5 milliseconds by default) to see if it can enter the room. If it still can’t enter because someone else is using the room (the lock is occupied), the transaction decides to stop and cancel its task entirely (this is called “aborting”).

Why Does This Happen? :
------------------------------ >

.> This behavior is there to prevent transactions from waiting forever for locks. It's a safety mechanism to avoid bottlenecks or situations where a transaction keeps waiting indefinitely and slows down the system.

.>.>  MongoDB locks are used to ensure data consistency while multiple operations are being executed. However, instead of locking the entire table or database, MongoDB locks only the specific document that's being modified.


EX :
====== >

...> Two people (transactions) are trying to update the same bank account at the same time. MongoDB places a "lock" on that specific account document so only one transaction can update it at a time. If the second transaction cannot get the lock within 5 milliseconds, it gives up and stops to avoid waiting forever.

.> Document-Level Locks: Operations only lock the specific document being accessed, allowing other transactions to interact with different documents simultaneously.

.> Timeout Behavior: If a lock can't be acquired (due to another operation holding it), MongoDB will retry for some time before throwing an error.



NOTE :
***********>

.> When creating or dropping a collection immediately before starting a transaction, if the collection is accessed within the transaction, issue the create or drop operation with write concern "majority" to ensure that the transaction can acquire the required locks.

