SETING UP THE REPLICA SETS IN THE MONGO DB :
==================================================>


.> When working with databases, it’s often useful to have multiple copies of your data. This provides redundancy in case one of the database servers fails and can improve a database’s availability and scalability, as well as reduce read latencies.

.> Replication is the process of duplicating or synchronizing data across multiple databases or servers. It ensures redundancy, high availability, and improved performance in case of server failure. In MongoDB, replication is achieved through replica sets.

.> The practice of synchronizing data across multiple separate databases is called replication. In MongoDB, a group of servers that maintain the same data set through replication are referred to as a replica set.

.> A replica set in MongoDB is a group of servers (or nodes) that maintain the same data set through replication. A replica set typically consists of:


Primary Node: Handles all write operations and replicates data to the secondary nodes.

Secondary Nodes: Receive replicated data from the primary node and serve read requests (if configured).

Arbiter Node (optional): Participates in elections for primary but does not hold data.


When setting up a MongoDB replica set, the primary node contains the original data, while the secondary nodes maintain copies of that same data through replication. So, the complete dataset is synchronized across all the servers in the replica set.

Here’s how it works:

The primary node handles all write operations and replicates the data to the secondary nodes.

The secondary nodes receive these updates and replicate the data from the primary. They serve as backup copies of the data for high availability and redundancy.

Secondary nodes do not store additional or different data; they store the same data as the primary node to ensure consistency across the replica set.

This mechanism ensures that, even if one server fails (e.g., the primary), the remaining servers (the secondary nodes) can take over seamlessly and continue to provide access to the same data.

Working flow here  in the Replica set in mongoDB :
================================================================= >

In a MongoDB replica set, all servers (nodes) in the set maintain the same databases, the same collections, and the same data within those collections.

Here's how it works in the case of your setup:

All three servers (Server1, Server2, Server3) will replicate the data from the primary server (Server1).

If you insert data into the CustomerDB database's Customers collection on the primary server, that exact data will automatically be replicated to the CustomerDB database's Customers collection on the secondary servers (Server2 and Server3).

Similarly, the OrderDB database's Orders collection will have identical data across all servers.

This replication ensures consistency among all nodes and provides fault tolerance—if the primary server fails, one of the secondary servers can take over as the new primary while still maintaining the same data.











Example: Setting up a Replica Set
Let’s assume we have three servers:

Server1 (192.168.1.10)

Server2 (192.168.1.20)

Server3 (192.168.1.30)

We also have two databases:

CustomerDB

OrderDB

Within these databases, there are collections:

CustomerDB: Customers

OrderDB: Orders

We want to set up a replica set named myReplicaSet to replicate the data across these servers.

Steps and Configuration
Start MongoDB Instances: Launch MongoDB instances on each server using the following commands:

bash
mongod --replSet "myReplicaSet" --port 27017 --dbpath /data/db1 --bind_ip 192.168.1.10
mongod --replSet "myReplicaSet" --port 27017 --dbpath /data/db2 --bind_ip 192.168.1.20
mongod --replSet "myReplicaSet" --port 27017 --dbpath /data/db3 --bind_ip 192.168.1.30
Connect to MongoDB Shell: Connect to the primary server (Server1) and initialize the replica set:

bash
mongo --host 192.168.1.10
Run the following command in the MongoDB shell:

javascript
rs.initiate({
   _id: "myReplicaSet",
   members: [
      { _id: 0, host: "192.168.1.10:27017" },
      { _id: 1, host: "192.168.1.20:27017" },
      { _id: 2, host: "192.168.1.30:27017" }
   ]
});
This sets up the replica set and designates Server1 as the Primary Node.

Insert Data into Primary: Now, insert sample data into collections. For example:

In CustomerDB:

javascript
use CustomerDB;

db.Customers.insertMany([
   { _id: 1, name: "John Doe", email: "john@example.com" },
   { _id: 2, name: "Jane Smith", email: "jane@example.com" }
]);
In OrderDB:

javascript
use OrderDB;

db.Orders.insertMany([
   { _id: 101, customerId: 1, total: 250 },
   { _id: 102, customerId: 2, total: 350 }
]);
Check Data Replication: Connect to one of the secondary servers (Server2 or Server3) using the MongoDB shell:

bash
mongo --host 192.168.1.20
Run the following commands to check replicated data:

javascript
use CustomerDB;
db.Customers.find();

use OrderDB;
db.Orders.find();
You should see the same data replicated from the primary node.

Benefits of Replica Sets
High Availability: If the primary server fails, a secondary server is automatically elected as the new primary.

Data Redundancy: Data is synchronized across all nodes.

Improved Read Performance: Secondary nodes can serve read requests (with readPreference configuration).

 




